<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kadazan-Dusun</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kadazan-Dusun are the largest Indigenous group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabah">Sabah</a>, the Malaysian state in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>, a collective name for the closely related Kadazan and Dusun peoples who speak Dusunic languages. Traditionally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farmers of the west coast and the interior, many became Christians under British North Borneo. Their harvest festival, Kaamatan, is a public holiday in Sabah.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kadazan-dusun.jpg" alt="Kadazan-Dusun" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rombituon · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Susumayau_Kadazan_Papar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Kadazan-Dusun number almost a million people, concentrated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabah">Sabah</a>'s West Coast Division, the upper Interior Division and Kudat Division, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sandakan">Sandakan</a> and Tawau divisions. Their tradition holds that they descend from the Nunuk Ragang, although not all the groups now counted as Kadazan-Dusun came from the same place. Other names they use for themselves are Mamasok and Momogun, meaning indigenous people and people of the land, sometimes combined as Pasok Momogun.</p><p>Dusun, meaning orchard people, was a Malay exonym used by the tax collectors of the Sultanate of Brunei for the agricultural peoples of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>; the Dusunic languages have no such word. Kadazan was the name used on the western coast, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kota-kinabalu">Jesselton</a>, Penampang, Papar and Beaufort, and was widely used by Roman Catholic missionaries from the 1880s. According to the Bobolian priestesses, Kadazan means people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The British North Borneo Chartered Company grouped all these peoples under a single Dusun classification, and an early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of British North Borneo counted the Dusuns as the largest group, almost half the population. Article 9 of the company's royal charter of 1 November 1881 required its courts to have careful regard to the customs and laws of each people, above all in land, inheritance and marriage. Roman Catholic missionaries of the Mill Hill mission in the late 19th century made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> prominent among the Kadazans of Penampang and Papar and most Dusuns of Tambunan. Towards the end of British rule, Kadazan-Dusun parties governed Sabah.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Kadazan-Dusun are known above all as farmers of wet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and hill paddy, and the Kaamatan festival honours the paddy's spirit, Bambarayon. Customary law, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adat">adat</a>, governs the gathering of forest produce and hunting while limiting over-exploitation, and the tagal system manages river fisheries. Their paramount leader is called Huguon Siou, brave leader.</p><p>Most are now Christians, mainly Roman Catholics, while some Dusuns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranau">Ranau</a> and the Bisaya of Beaufort, Kuala Penyu and Sipitang practise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>; before conversion they followed a traditional religion, Momolianism, and practised headhunting, as did the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murut">Muruts</a>. Music centres on the gong and the kulintangan, and the main dance of the Penampang Kadazan, the Sumazau, is performed by a man and a woman to seven or eight gongs.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kadazan-Dusun” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Of some 34 million people, roughly seven in ten citizens are Bumiputera, Malays and the indigenous peoples of Sabah and Sarawak such as the Iban and <b>Kadazan-Dusun</b>, about a quarter are Chinese and less than a tenth are of Indian descent, mostly Tamil.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malaysia">Malaysia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Local resistance, notably the rebellion of Mat Salleh from 1894 to 1900, was suppressed, and indigenous peoples such as the <b>Kadazan-Dusun</b> and Murut faced new taxes.</blockquote><a href="/state/north-borneo">North Borneo</a></li></ul></section>
